== Part II: An Humble Spirit with the Lowly

#align(center)[Prov 16:19.]

#align(center)[#emph[Better to be of a humble spirit with the lowly than to divide the spoil with the proud.]]

If men could once be brought to believe that it is better to have their minds brought to bend to the crook in their lot than to force the crook to be made even to suit their mind, they would then be in a fair way to bring their affairs in that case to a good outcome. Here, then, is the divine decision in that case: “Better to be of a humble spirit with the lowly than to divide the spoil with the proud.” In these words,

#emph[First,] there is a comparison set up, and that between two parties, and two points in which they differ vastly.

#emph[1st.] The parties are the #emph[lowly] and the #emph[proud,] who differ like heaven and the center of the earth. The #emph[proud] are always climbing up and soaring aloft; the #emph[lowly] are content to creep on the ground, if that is the will of God. Let us view them more particularly as the text represents them.

(1.) On the one hand is the #emph[lowly.] Here there is a reading in the line of the text and one in the margin, both from the Holy Spirit, and they differ only in one letter. The former is the afflicted or poor, who are low in their condition: those who have a notable crook in their lot through affliction laid on them, by which their condition in the world is lowered. The other is the lowly, or the meek and humble ones, who are low in their spirit as well as in their condition, and so have their mind brought down to their lot. Both together make up the character of this lowly party.

(2.) On the other hand is the #emph[proud,] the showy and high-minded ones. It is supposed here that they too are crossed and have crooks in their lot; for dividing the spoil follows a victory, and a victory presupposes a battle.

#emph[2d.] The points in which these parties are supposed to differ, namely, being of a #emph[humble spirit,] and #emph[dividing the spoil.]

Afflicted and lowly ones may sometimes have their condition changed. They may be raised up on high and divide the spoil, as Hannah, Job and others were. The proud may sometimes be thrown down and crushed, as Pharaoh, Nebuchadnezzar and others were. But that is not the question, whether it is better to be raised up with the #emph[lowly] or thrown down with the #emph[proud.] There would be no difficulty in deciding that. The question is this: is it better to be of a low and humble spirit, in low circumstances, with afflicted humble ones, or to divide the spoil and get one’s will with the proud? If men would speak the natural feelings of their hearts, that question would be decided against the text. The points compared here, and set one against the other, are these:

(1.) On the one hand, to be of a #emph[humble spirit] with afflicted #emph[lowly] ones. The Hebrew is #emph[to be of low spirit,] for the word first of all denotes lowness in position or state. So the point proposed here is to be with afflicted lowly ones, or in their state, having the spirit brought down to that low lot, the lowness of the spirit balancing the lowness of one’s condition.

(2.) On the other hand, to #emph[divide the spoil with the proud.] The point proposed here is to be with the proud, or in their state, having their lot brought by main force to their liking, like those who, believing themselves wronged, fight it out with the enemy, overcome, and divide the spoil as they please.

2\. The decision made, in which the former is preferred to the latter: “Better …” If these two parties were set before us, it would be better to take our lot with those of a low condition who have their spirits brought as low as their lot, than with those who, being of a proud and high-bent spirit, have their lot brought up to their liking. A humbled spirit is better than a heightened condition.

=== Doctrine.

#emph[There is a generation of lowly afflicted ones, whose spirit is lowered and brought down to their lot, and whose case, in that respect, is better than that of the proud who get their will and carry everything to their liking.]

I. We shall consider the generation of the lowly afflicted ones, whose spirit is brought down to their lot. And we shall, #emph[1st,] lay down some general considerations about them.

1\. There is such a generation in the world, bad as the world is. The text expressly mentions them, and Scripture mentions them elsewhere, as in Ps 9:12 and 10:12, and Matt 5:3 with Luke 6:20. Where shall we look for them? Not in heaven: there are no afflicted ones there. Not in hell: there are no lowly or humble ones there, whose spirit is brought to their lot. So they must be in this world, where the state of trial is.

2\. If it were not so, Christ, as he was in the world, would have no followers in it. He was the Head of that generation, whom they all copy: “Learn from Me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart” (Matt 11:29). And for his honor, and the honor of his cross, they will never be lacking as long as the world stands: “For those God foreknew, He also predestined to be conformed to the image of His Son” (Rom 8:29). His image lies in these two things, #emph[suffering] and #emph[holiness,] of which #emph[lowliness] is a chief part.

3\. Nevertheless they are certainly very rare in the world. Agur observes that there is another generation, quite opposite to them: “how haughty are their eyes and pretentious are their glances” (Prov 30:13). And this makes up by far the greatest company. The low and afflicted lot is not so very rare, but the lowly disposition of spirit is rarely yoked with it. Many a high-bent spirit stays bent high in spite of lowering circumstances.

4\. They can be no more in number than the truly godly. For nothing less than the power of divine grace can bring men’s minds down from their natural height and make their will pliant to the will of God (2 Cor 10:4–5). Men may put on a face of submission to a low and crossed lot because they cannot help it and see that it is useless to strive. But to bring the spirit truly to it must be the work of humbling grace.

5\. Though all the godly are of that generation, there are some of them to whom that character belongs more especially. The way to heaven lies through tribulation for all (Acts 14:22), and all Christ’s followers are reconciled to it nonetheless (Luke 14:26). Yet some of them are more remarkably disciplined than others, and their spirit is nevertheless humbled by it and brought down to their lot: “Surely I have stilled and quieted my soul; like a weaned child with his mother, like a weaned child is my soul within me” (Ps 131:2). “For I have learned to be content regardless of my circumstances. I know how to live humbly, and I know how to abound. I am accustomed to any and every situation—to being filled and being hungry, to having plenty and having need” (Phil 4:11–12).

#emph[Lastly,] a lowly disposition of soul, and the habitual aim and bent of the heart that way, is very favorably regarded in heaven. If we looked for a generation perfectly purged of pride, and of the heart’s risings against their adverse lot at any time, we would find none in this world. But those who sincerely aim and endeavor to reach it, and to keep the way of contented submission, returning to it again though they are sometimes blown aside, God counts to be that lowly generation (2 Cor 8:12; Jas 5:11).

II. We shall go into the particulars of their character. Three things together make up their character.

#emph[First,] affliction in their lot. That lowly generation, preferred to the proud and prosperous, is a generation of afflicted ones, whom God keeps under the discipline of the covenant. We may sum it up in these two things.

1\. A yoke of affliction of one kind or another is often upon them (Ps 73:14). If there is silence in heaven, it is only for half an hour (Rev 8:1). God is often visiting them, as a master visits his pupils and a physician his patients, whereas others are in a way overlooked by him (Rev 3:19). They are used to the yoke, and that from the time they enter God’s family (Ps 129:1–3). God sees that it is good for them (Lam 3:27–28).

2\. There is a particular yoke of affliction which God has chosen for them. It hangs about them, and is seldom, if ever, off them (Luke 9:23). That is their special trial, the crook in their lot, the yoke which lies on them for their constant exercise. Their other trials may be changed, but that is a weight that hangs about them continually, bowing them down.

#emph[Secondly,] lowliness in their disposition and the tenor of their spirit. They are a generation of lowly, humble ones, whose spirits God has by his grace brought down from their natural height. And so:

1\. They think soberly and humbly of themselves: of what they are (2 Cor 12:11), what they can do (2 Cor 3:5), what they are worth (Gen 32:10), and what they deserve (Lam 3:22). Viewing themselves in the mirror of the divine law and perfection, they see themselves as a mass of imperfection and sinfulness (Job 42:5–6).

2\. They think highly and honorably of God (Ps 144:3). They are taught by the Spirit what God is, and so they have exalted thoughts of him. They consider him as the Sovereign of the world, his perfections as infinite, his work as perfect. They look on him as the fountain of happiness, as a God in Christ who does all things well, and they trust his wisdom, goodness and love even where they cannot see (Heb 11:8).

3\. They think favorably of others, as far as justice allows (Phil 2:3). Though they cannot keep themselves from seeing others’ glaring faults, they are ready at the same time to acknowledge their excellencies, and to esteem them for these as far as they go. And because they see more into their own mercies and advantages for holiness, and their own misuse of them, than they can see into those of others, they are inclined to look on others as better than themselves, when circumstances are compared.

4\. They are sunk down into a state of subordination to God and his will (Ps 131:1–2). Pride sets a man up against God. Lowliness brings him back to his place and lays him down at the feet of his sovereign Lord, saying, “Your will be done, on earth …” They no longer seek the command, but are content that God himself should sit at the helm of their affairs and manage everything for them (Ps 47:4).

5\. They are not bent on high things, but disposed to stoop to low things (Ps 131:1). Lowliness levels the towering imaginations which pride raises up against heaven. It draws a veil over all personal worth and excellencies before the Lord, and it yields a man’s all to the Lord, to serve as stepping stones to the throne of his glory (2 Sam 15:25–26).

#emph[Lastly,] they are inclined to magnify the mercies given to them (Gen 32:10). Pride of heart overlooks and belittles the mercies one has, and fixes the eye on what is lacking in one’s condition. It makes one like the flies that pass over the sound places and swarm together on the sore. Lowliness, on the contrary, teaches men to count up the mercies they enjoy in the lowest condition, and to set a mark on the good things they have had, or still have (Job 2:10).

#emph[Thirdly,] a spirit brought down to their lot. Their lot is a low and afflicted one, but their spirit is as low, being brought down to it through grace. We may sum it up in these five things:

1\. They submit to it as just: “Because I have sinned against Him, I must endure the rage of the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Mic 7:9). There are no hardships in our condition that we have not brought on ourselves. So it is just that we kiss the rod and are silent under it, and so lower our spirits to our lot. If they complain, their complaints are against themselves. Their hearts do not rise up against the Lord; still less do they open their mouths against the heavens. They justify God and condemn themselves, revering his holiness and spotless righteousness in his proceedings against them.

2\. They go quietly under it as bearable: “It is good to wait quietly for the salvation of the #smallcaps[Lord]. It is good for a man to bear the yoke while he is still young. Let him sit alone in silence, for God has disciplined him. Let him bury his face in the dust—perhaps there is still hope” (Lam 3:26–29). While the unsubdued spirit rages under the yoke like a bullock unaccustomed to it, the spirit brought to the lot goes softly under it. They see that it is of the Lord’s mercy that it is not worse. They take up the bare cross as God lays it down, without the extra weights that turbulent passions add to it. And so it really becomes easier than they thought it could have been, like a burden fitted to the back.

3\. They are satisfied in it, drawing their comfort from another quarter than their outward condition, just as the house stands firm when a prop it did not lean on is taken away. “Though the fig tree does not bud and no fruit is on the vines, … yet I will exult in the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Hab 3:17–18). So David did in the day of his distress: he “found strength in the #smallcaps[Lord] his God” (1 Sam 30:6). It is a sign of a spirit not brought down to the lot when one is dejected and sunk under its hardships, as if one’s condition in the world were the point on which one’s happiness turned. It is the lack of mortification that makes men’s comforts wax and wane, ebb and flow, according to the various appearances of their lot in the world.

4\. They take delight in it, as what is fit and good for them (Isa 39:8; 2 Cor 12:10). Men have a sort of satisfaction in the working of medicine, though it gripes them sorely: they reason with themselves that it is good and best for them. So these lowly souls regard their afflicted lot as a spiritual medicine, necessary, fit and good for them, yes, best for them for the time, since it is administered by their heavenly Father. And so they reach a holy delight in their low afflicted lot.

The lowly spirit draws this sweetness out of the bitterness in the lot. It considers how the Lord, by means of that afflicting lot, stops the supply for unruly lusts, so that they may be starved; how he cuts off the side channels, so that the whole stream of the soul’s love may run toward himself; how he pulls off and holds off the man’s burden and hindrance of earthly comforts, so that he may run the more swiftly in the way to heaven.

#emph[Lastly,] they rest in it, as what they do not wish to come out of until the God who brought them into it sees fit to bring them out with his good will (Isa 28:16). Though an unsubdued spirit’s time for deliverance is always ready, a humbled soul will be afraid of being taken out of its afflicted lot too soon. It will not push for a change until heaven moves to bring it about. Yet this does not hinder prayer and the use of appointed means, with dependence on the Lord, but requires faith, hope, patience and resignation (2 Sam 15:25–26).

II. We shall consider the generation of the #emph[proud] who get their will and carry everything to their liking. In their character also there are three things.

#emph[First,] crosses in their lot. They too have their trials allotted to them by overruling providence, and whatever circumstances in the world they may be in, they cannot escape them altogether. For consider:

1\. The confusion and vanity brought into the creation by man’s sin have made it impossible to get through the world without meeting what will ruffle men (Eccl 1:14). Sin has turned the world from a paradise into a thicket; there is no getting through without being scratched. As the midges in summer will fly about those walking outdoors in fine clothes as well as about those in shabby clothes, so crosses in the world will meet the high as well as the low.

2\. The pride of their heart exposes them particularly to crosses. A proud heart will make a cross for itself where a lowly soul would find none (Esth 5:13). It will make a real cross ten times heavier than it would be to the humble. The generation of the proud are like nettles and thorn-hedges, on which things flying about settle, while they pass over low and plain things. So none are more exposed to crosses than they are, though none are so unfit to bear them, as appears from what follows:

#emph[Secondly,] reigning pride in their spirit. Their spirits were never subdued by a work of thorough humiliation; they remain at the height where the corruption of nature set them. That is why they can in no way bear the yoke God lays on them. The neck is swollen with the infection of pride and passion, so when the yoke once begins to touch it, they can have no more ease. We may view the case of the proud generation here in three things.

1\. They value themselves too highly, and so the proud mind says the man should not stoop to the yoke; it is beneath him. What swelling vanity there is in this: “Who is the #smallcaps[Lord] that I should obey His voice?” (Exod 5:2). That is why a work of humiliation is necessary to make one take on the yoke, whether of #emph[Christ’s] precepts or of his providence. The first error is in the understanding, which is why Solomon commonly calls a wicked man a fool. Accordingly the first stroke in conversion falls there too, humbling by conviction. Men are bigger in their own conceit than they really are. So God, suiting things to what we really are, cannot please us.

2\. They have an unmortified self-will, arising from that overvaluing of themselves, and it says he will not stoop (Exod 5:2). The question between Heaven and us is whether God’s will or our own must win the day. Our will is corrupt; God’s will is holy; they cannot agree. God says in his providence that our will must yield to his. But our will will not do that until the iron sinew in it is broken (Rom 6:7; Isa 48:4).

3\. They have a crowd of unsubdued passions taking the side of self-will, and these say he shall not stoop (Rom 7:8–9). And so the war begins, and there is a field of battle within the man and outside him (Jas 4:1).

#emph[1st,] a holy God crosses the self-will of proud creatures by his providence, overruling and disposing of things contrary to their inclination. Sometimes he does it by his own immediate hand, as in the case of Cain (Gen 4:4–5); sometimes by the hand of men who carry things against their wishes, as in the case of Ahab, to whom Naboth refused his vineyard (1 Kgs 21:4).

#emph[2dly,] the proud heart and will, unable to submit to the cross or to bear being controlled, rises up against it and fights for the mastery with its whole force of unmortified passions. The aim is to remove the cross, that is, the crook, and to bring the thing to their own liking. This is the cause of this unholy war, in which:

(1.) One black band of hellish passions marches upward and attacks Heaven itself, namely discontent, impatience, murmuring, fretting and the like: “A man’s own folly subverts his way, yet his heart rages against the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Prov 19:3). These set the breast on fire and make the face fall (Gen 4:5). Sometimes they let off a volley of unseemly and passionate complaints (Jude 16), and sometimes of blasphemies (2 Kgs 6:33).

(2.) Another band marches forward and attacks the instrument or instruments of the cross, namely anger, wrath, fury, revenge, bitterness and the like (Prov 27:4). These carry the man out of the possession of himself (Luke 21:19). They fill the heart with a boiling heat (Ps 39:3) and the mouth with clamor and evil-speaking (Eph 4:31). Threats are breathed out (Acts 9:1), and sometimes they set the hands to work, with a most grievous outcome (Matt 10:21–22), as in the case of Ahab against Naboth.

So the proud carry on the war, but often they lose the day, and the cross remains immovable for all they can do. Yes, and sometimes they themselves fall in the quarrel, and it ends in their ruin (Exod 15:9–10). But that is not the case in the text. We are to consider them as:

#emph[Thirdly,] getting their will and carrying everything to their liking. This speaks of:

1\. Holy providence yielding to the man’s unmortified self-will, and letting things go as he wishes (Gen 6:3). God sees fit to let his striving with him drop, for it does not prevail for his good (Isa 1:5). So the reins are laid on the proud man’s neck, and he has what he was after: “Ephraim is joined to idols; leave him alone!” (Hos 4:17).

2\. The lust remaining in its strength and vigor: “They were not turned from their lust” (Ps 78:30). God, in the way of his covenant, sometimes gives his people their will and sets them where they would be. But then, in that case, the lust for the thing is mortified, and they are like weaned children (Ps 10:17). But here the lust remains rampant; the proud seek food for it, and get it.

3\. The cross removed and the yoke taken off (Ps 78:29). They could not think of bringing their mind to their lot, but opposed it, wrestled and fought against it, until it was brought up to their liking. So the day is theirs; the victory is on their side.

4\. #emph[Lastly,] the man is pleased at having carried his point, just as one is when he is dividing the spoil (1 Kgs 21:18–19).

Thus the case of the afflicted lowly generation, and of the proud generation prospering, is stated. Now,

III. I am to confirm the doctrine, or the decision of the text: that the case of the former is better than that of the latter. It is better to be in a low afflicted condition, with the spirit humbled and brought down to the lot, than to be of a proud and high spirit, having the lot brought up to it and matters going according to one’s will and wish, as one likes. This will appear from the following considerations:

#emph[First,] humility is so far preferable to pride that in no circumstances whatever can its preference fail. Let all the afflictions in the world attend the humble spirit, and all the prosperity in the world attend pride, humility will still have the better of it, as gold in a dunghill is more excellent than the same amount of lead in a cabinet. For:

1\. Humility is a part of the image of God. Pride is the masterpiece of the image of the devil. Let us look at him who was the express image of the Father’s person, and we shall see him meek and lowly in heart (Matt 11:29). None was more afflicted, yet his spirit was perfectly brought down to his lot: “He was oppressed and afflicted, yet He did not open His mouth” (Isa 53:7). That is a shining part of the divine image. For though God cannot be low in his state and condition, yet he is of infinite condescension (Isa 57:15). None forbears as he does (Rom 2:4), nor patiently suffers so much opposition to his will. This is set before us for our encouragement in affliction, as it shone in Christ: “Consider Him who endured such hostility from sinners, so that you#super[+] will not grow weary and lose heart” (Heb 12:3).

Pride, on the other hand, is the very image of the devil (1 Tim 3:6). Will we pride ourselves on the height of our spirits? Satan will compete with the highest of us on that point. For though he is the most miserable, he is the proudest in the whole creation. There is the greatest distance between his spirit and his lot: the one is as high as the throne of God, the other as low as hell. And as it is impossible that his lot should ever be brought up to his spirit, so his spirit will never come down to his lot. Therefore he will be eternally at war with his lot. That is why, even now, he has no rest, but goes about seeking rest, and finds none.

Now, is it not better to be like God than like the devil? Like him who is the fountain of all good, rather than him who is the spring and sink of all evil? Can anything possibly tip the balance here and turn the preference to the other side? Then “better to be of a humble spirit with the lowly …”

2\. Humility and lowliness of spirit fit us for friendly communion and fellowship with God in Christ. Pride makes God our enemy (1 Pet 5:5). Our happiness here and hereafter depends on our friendly fellowship with heaven. If we do not have that, nothing can make up our loss (Ps 30:5). If we have it, nothing can make us miserable: “If God is for us, who can be against us?” (Rom 8:31). Now, who are those whom God is for, but the humble and lowly? They are those who, being in Christ, are in this made like him. He blesses them and declares them heirs to the crown of glory: “Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven” (Matt 5:3). He will look to them, however low their condition may be, while he overlooks others (Isa 66:2). He will have regard for them, however they may be despised: “Though the #smallcaps[Lord] is on high, He attends to the lowly; but the proud He knows from afar” (Ps 138:6). He will dwell with them, however poorly they dwell (Isa 57:15). He will certainly exalt them in due time, however low they lie now (Isa 40:4).

Whom is he against? Whom does he resist? The proud. Them he curses (Jer 17:5), and that curse will at length dry up their arm. The proud man is God’s rival. He makes himself his own god, and would have those around him make him theirs too. He rages and blusters if they will not fall down before him. But God will bring him down (Isa 40:4; Ps 18:27).

Now, is it not better to be fit for communion with God than to have him set against us on any terms?

3\. Humility is a duty pleasing to God; pride is a sin pleasing to the devil (Isa 57:15; 1 Tim 3:6). God requires us to be humble, especially under affliction: “… clothe yourselves with humility” (1 Pet 5:5–6). That is our fitting garment. The humble tax collector was accepted, the proud Pharisee rejected. We may say of the generation of the proud, as in 1 Thess 2:16, “the utmost wrath has come upon them.” They please neither God nor men, but only themselves and Satan, whom they resemble in this. Now, duty is always better than sin, on any terms.

#emph[Secondly,] those whose spirits are brought down to their afflicted lot have much quiet and rest of mind, while the proud, who must have their lot brought up to their liking, have much unrest, trouble and vexation. Consider here, on the one hand:

1\. Quiet of mind and ease within is a great blessing, on which the comfort of life depends. Nothing outside us can make one’s life happy without it (Dan 5:6). And where it is kept, nothing can make life miserable (John 16:33). When this is secured in God, defiance is given to all the troubles of the world (Ps 46:2–4), like the child sailing in the midst of the rolling waves.

2\. The spirit brought down to the lot creates and keeps this inward calm. All our trouble in our lot in the world rises from our mind’s disagreement with it. Let the mind be brought to the lot, and the whole tumult is instantly hushed. Let it be kept in that disposition, and the man will stand at ease in his affliction, like a rock unmoved by the waters beating on it: “Let the peace of Christ rule in your#super[+] hearts, for to this you were called” (Col 3:15).

On the other hand, consider:

1\. What disquiet of mind the proud suffer before they can get their lot brought up to their liking. “They have taught their tongues to lie; they wear themselves out committing iniquity” (Jer 9:5). “You#super[+] crave what you do not have; you kill and covet, but are unable to obtain it. You#super[+] quarrel and fight. You#super[+] do not have …” (Jas 4:2). What arrows of grief go through their heart? What torture of anxiety, fretting and vexation must they endure? What contrary passions fight within them, and what outbursts of passion do they make? What unease Haman was in before he could carry his point of revenge against Mordecai by obtaining the king’s decree!

2\. When the thing is got as they wished, it will not be worth the cost. Having it does not bring as much satisfaction and pleasure as the lack of it gives pain. This was evident in Rachel’s case as to having children, and in the case of Ps 78:30–31. There is a dead fly in the ointment that spoils the fragrance they expected to find in it. Fruit plucked from the tree of providence before it is ripe will readily set the teeth on edge. It proves like the manna kept overnight (Exod 16:20).

3\. They have no sure hold on it; it does not last with them. Either it is soon taken from them, and they are just where they were before: “In My anger I gave you a king, and in My wrath I took him away” (Hos 13:11). Having a root of pride, it quickly withers away. Or else they are taken from it, so that they have no opportunity to enjoy it. So Haman obtained the decree, but before the day of its execution came he was gone.

#emph[Thirdly,] those who get their spirit brought down to their afflicted lot gain a point far more valuable than those who in their pride force their lot up to their liking: “He who is slow to anger is better than a warrior, and he who rules his spirit is greater than one who captures a city” (Prov 16:32). This will appear if we consider:

1\. The latter makes only a better condition in outward things; the former makes a better man. Life is more than food. The man himself is more valuable than all the outward conveniences that attend him. So what betters the man is preferable to what betters only his condition. Suppose two are sick. One has himself carried from a coarse bed to a fine one, but his sickness remains. The other lies still in the coarse bed, but his sickness is removed. Who doubts that the case of the latter is preferable? So it is here.

2\. The subduing of our passions is more excellent than having the whole world subdued to our will. For then we are masters of ourselves, as Luke 21:19 has it, whereas in the other case we are still slaves to the worst of masters (Rom 6:16). In the one case we are safe, whatever storm may blow; in the other we lie exposed to thousands of dangers: “Like a city whose walls are broken down is a man who does not rule his spirit” (Prov 25:28).

#emph[Lastly,] when both come to be judged, it will appear that the one has multiplied the tally of his good works by bringing his spirit to his lot, and the other the tally of his evil works by bringing his lot to his spirit. We have to do with an all-knowing God, in whose eyes every inward action is a work, good or bad, to be accounted for (Rom 2:16).

An afflicted lot is painful, but where it is well managed it is very fruitful. It exercises the graces of the Spirit in a Christian, which would otherwise lie dormant. But there is never an act of resignation to the will of God under the cross, nor an act of trusting in him for his help, that will not be recorded in Heaven’s register as a good work (Mal 3:16). And these are occasioned by affliction.

On the other hand, there is never a rising of the proud heart against the lot, nor a faithless attempt to bring the lot to our liking, whether it succeeds or not, that does not pass for an evil work before God. How greatly, then, will the tally of such works be multiplied by the war in which this spoil is divided!

USE I. Of information. From this we may learn:

1\. It is not always best for people to get their will. There are many who cannot be pleased with God’s will for them, and they get their own will with a vengeance: “Israel would not obey Me. So I gave them up to their stubborn hearts to follow their own devices” (Ps 81:11–12). It may be the most pleasant and welcome for the time, but it is not the safest. So let people not pride themselves on carrying things that way by a strong hand; let them not triumph in such a victory. The reckoning afterward will open their eyes.

2\. The afflicted, crossed party, whose lot is kept low, is so far from being a loser by it that he is a gainer, if his spirit is brought down to it. And if he will look at his case in the light of God’s unerring word, he is better off than if he had got everything carried his way. In the one way the vessels of wrath are fitted for destruction (Ps 78:29–31). In the other the vessels of mercy are fitted for glory, and so God disciplines his own (Lam 3:27).

3\. It is better to yield to providence than to fight it out, even if we should win. Yielding to the sovereign disposal is both our fitting duty and our greatest interest. Taking that way, we act most honorably: for what honor can there be in a creature disputing his ground with his Creator? And we act most wisely: for whatever the success of some battles in that case, we may be sure the victory in the war will be on Heaven’s side: “for by his own strength shall no man prevail” (1 Sam 2:9).

#emph[Lastly,] it is of far greater concern for us to get our spirits brought down than to get our outward condition raised. But who believes this? All men strive to raise their outward condition; most men never give a thought to bringing down their spirits, and few apply themselves to it. And what is that but to be concerned to give drink to the thirsty sick, but never to think of seeking a cure for them that would carry off their thirst?

USE II. As you#super[+] meet with crosses in your lot in the world, let your bent be rather to get your spirit humbled and brought down than to get the cross removed. I do not mean that you#super[+] may not use all lawful means for the removal of your cross, in dependence on God, but only that you should be more concerned to get your spirit to bow and bend than to get the crook in your lot made even.

#emph[Motive] 1. It is far more needful for us to have our spirits humbled under the cross than to have the cross removed. The removal of the cross is needful only for the ease of the flesh; the humbling is needful for the profit of our souls, to purify them and bring them into a state of health and cure.

2\. The humbling of the spirit will have a very good effect on a crossed lot, but the removal of the cross will have none on the unhumbled spirit. The humbling will lighten the cross greatly for the time (Matt 11:30), and in due time carry it clean off (1 Pet 5:6). But the removal of the cross is no means of humbling the unhumbled. Though it may prevent irritation, the disease still remains.

#emph[Lastly,] think with yourselves how dangerous and hopeless a case it is to have the cross removed before the spirit is humbled. That is to have the means of cure pulled away and blocked up from us while the power of the disease is still unbroken. It is to be taken off trials before we have given any good proof of ourselves, and so to be given up by our physician as hopeless (Isa 1:5; Hos 4:17).

For direction: believing the gospel, take God for your#super[+] God in Christ toward your eternal salvation. Then dwell much on thoughts of God’s greatness and holiness, and of your#super[+] own sinfulness. So you#super[+] will be humbled under the mighty hand of God, and in due time he will lift you up.
